From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: mingmingzhichu <lihaoyu0717@outlook.com>
Date: Tue, 10 Nov 2026 20:01:00 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] regulator: rpmh: allow platforms to skip voltage/state
 readback

Some firmware configurations do not hand the RPMh read channel to the OS.
On those platforms every rpmh_read() times out, and because a timed-out
request keeps its TCS busy, all later RPMh transactions fail as well -
including BCM votes.  The result is a set of apparently unrelated driver
failures during boot, and the stale TCS can prevent the boot from
progressing at all.

Add an opt-in "qcom,no-readback" property.  When set, the driver reports
the cached voltage selector and skips the initial PMIC-mode probe instead
of issuing read requests.  The property defaults to off, so existing
platforms are unaffected.

Signed-off-by: mingmingzhichu <lihaoyu0717@outlook.com>
---
 drivers/regulator/qcom-rpmh-regulator.c | 23 +++++++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 23 insertions(+)

diff --git a/drivers/regulator/qcom-rpmh-regulator.c b/drivers/regulator/qcom-rpmh-regulator.c
--- a/drivers/regulator/qcom-rpmh-regulator.c
+++ b/drivers/regulator/qcom-rpmh-regulator.c
@@ -167,6 +167,7 @@ struct rpmh_vreg {
 	struct regulator_desc		rdesc;
 	const struct rpmh_vreg_hw_data	*hw_data;
 	bool				always_wait_for_ack;
+	bool				skip_readback;
 
 	int				enabled;
 	bool				bypassed;
@@ -276,6 +277,13 @@ static int rpmh_regulator_vrm_get_voltage_sel(struct regulator_dev *rdev)
 	struct rpmh_vreg *vreg = rdev_get_drvdata(rdev);
 	int ret, uV = 0;
 
+	/*
+	 * Some firmware leaves the RPMH read path unusable.  On those
+	 * platforms the DT can request cached-only behaviour instead.
+	 */
+	if (vreg->skip_readback)
+		return vreg->voltage_selector;
+
 	if (vreg->voltage_selector < 0) {
 		ret = _rpmh_regulator_vrm_get_voltage(rdev, &uV);
 		if (!ret && uV != 0)
@@ -551,6 +559,15 @@ static int rpmh_regulator_init_vreg(struct rpmh_vreg *vreg, struct device *dev,
 
 	vreg->always_wait_for_ack = of_property_read_bool(node,
 						"qcom,always-wait-for-ack");
+	/*
+	 * The quirk is normally placed on the regulators container node,
+	 * because that is the device which describes the PMIC.  Also accept
+	 * it on an individual regulator for compatibility.
+	 */
+	vreg->skip_readback = of_property_read_bool(node, "qcom,no-readback") ||
+			      (node->parent &&
+			       of_property_read_bool(node->parent,
+						     "qcom,no-readback"));
 
 	vreg->rdesc.owner	= THIS_MODULE;
 	vreg->rdesc.type	= REGULATOR_VOLTAGE;
@@ -595,6 +612,9 @@ static int rpmh_regulator_determine_initial_mode(struct rpmh_vreg *vreg)
 	int ret, pmic_mode, mode;
 	int sts;
 
+	if (vreg->skip_readback)
+		return 0;
+
 	ret = rpmh_read(vreg->dev, &cmd);
 	if (ret) {
 		dev_err(vreg->dev, "failed to read ENABLE status ret = %d\n", ret);
@@ -1948,6 +1968,9 @@ static int rpmh_regulator_probe(struct platform_device *pdev)
 		return ret;
 	}
 
+	if (of_property_read_bool(dev->of_node, "qcom,no-readback"))
+		dev_info(dev, "RPMH readback disabled by device tree\n");
+
 	for_each_available_child_of_node_scoped(dev->of_node, node) {
 		vreg = devm_kzalloc(dev, sizeof(*vreg), GFP_KERNEL);
 		if (!vreg)
-- 
